In the recycling center, there are n trash bags, the i-th bag has a weight of ai. At each second, two actions will happen successively:

First, you must choose a trash bag and destroy it. It will cost 1 coin if the weight of the trash bag is strictly greater than c, and it will cost 0 coins otherwise.
Then, the weight of each remaining trash bag will get multiplied by two.
What is the minimum number of coins you have to spend to get rid of all trash bags?

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t (1≤t≤1000). The description of the test cases follows.
The first line of each test case contains two integers n and c (1≤n≤30, 1≤c≤10^9).
The second line of each test case contains n integers a1,a2,…,an (1≤ai≤10^9) — the weight of each trash bag.

Output:
For each test case, you must output a single integer — the minimum number of coins you have to spend to destroy all trash bags.

Strategy:
We can divide the numbers up into the ones we can try to save and the ones that are definitely lost. The ones that are lost are the ones > c since they will never be smaller and always incur a cost. We save that for last. Then there are numbers which we could save, we need to choose the order. The optimal way is always choosing the biggest one that is <= c since that's the one that will definitely not be able to get saved next round. Since n<=30 we just run a for loop for n iteration and sort the array, change the biggest <= c number to INF (found by upper_bound--) and double everything else. We continue like this until upper_bound doesnt find any number. Now all the numbers which are not INF are the ones that incue a cost. We output their count. We use ll for values to avoid overflow.